==English==

===Etymology===
Contraction of ''[[Saint]] [[Anthony]]'s [[pig]]'' in honour of St Anthony, patron saint of swineherds, frequently shown with a little pig by his side.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|slang}} The favourite or smallest pig in the litter.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1835|author=Wystan Hugh Auden|title=O What Is That Sound Which So Thrills The Ear|passage=And to follow the delicious breeze like a '''tantony pig''' .}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1838|author=Benjamin Webster|title=Acting National Drama|passage=I'm not going to tankety-tankety after his tail, like a '''Tantony pig''' any more.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1984|author=Richard Fitzneale|title=Dialogus De Scaccario: The Course of the Exchequer and Constitutio Domus Regis (the Establishment of the Royal Household)|passage=This system (which had the disadvantage of discouraging good farming, since the worst animal — the''' 'Tantony pig' '''— counted for as much as the best) was only applicable when the court was in constant progress.}}
#* '''2002''', Murray J Levith, ''Shakespeare's ''Merchant'' and Marlowe's Other Play'' in ''The Merchant of Venice: Critical Essays'', ed John Mahon, Ellen MacLeod Mahon
#*: A “Tantony pig” is the smallest of a litter, a detail that might recall Antonio's description of himself in Shakespeare's play as the “tainted wether of the flock".

